1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:12,640
I could definitely see a future, very scary future, in which a lot of people would not have meaningful work. This is Darren Asimoglu, an MIT economist and winner of the 2024 Nobel Prize in Economics.

2
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:19,280
He thinks we're overlooking some of AI's biggest opportunities. So where should we be looking and what will keep us valuable?

3
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:28,800
I've seen a lot of posts where people say, oh, instead of hiring someone, I deployed an agent. Automation is great. The problem is the only thing we do is automation.

4
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:38,640
That will eliminate too many jobs without creating meaningful, high-paying, high productivity jobs to create new opportunities for people. So what should people be doing?

5
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,040
What skills should they be acquiring? If anybody wants my opinion, I would say go and study.

6
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,040
Thanks to HubSpot for sponsoring this video.

7
00:00:48,494 --> 00:00:58,000
So you said something in 2024 that AI would add about 1% to US GDP in the next 10 years, with only 5% of tasks being fully automatable.

8
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,720
What do you think about this now in 2026?

9
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,840
Well, first of all, it's my pleasure to be here.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,640
And I do not claim that I have any unique insight into AI.

11
00:01:10,958 --> 00:01:13,860
AI is like an elephant, everybody is feeling a different part of it.

12
00:01:14,958 --> 00:01:16,000
But I think

13
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:20,420
I try to bring a little bit of basic

14
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:27,760
economic calculus to the discussions of productivity. And this was more than two years ago.

15
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:29,924
There's a huge amount of uncertainty.

16
00:01:30,606 --> 00:01:38,404
And there is even more today. But there have also been some very rapid developments, especially this year, with agentic AI.

17
00:01:39,086 --> 00:01:42,960
Probably now I would have put that number a little higher than 5%.

18
00:01:43,854 --> 00:01:52,400
But I still think that a lot of the discussion exaggerates how quickly we're going to see automation

19
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,404
because automation is hard.

20
00:01:54,894 --> 00:01:57,892
Integrating automation into organizations is hard.

21
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:06,400
Diffusion of new technologies, especially when they require lots of complementary investments and adjustments, is hard and slow.

22
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:09,600
That doesn't mean it's not a transformative technology.

23
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:10,628
It doesn't mean

24
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,732
that we cannot get benefits from it.

25
00:02:13,966 --> 00:02:14,756
But we have to be

26
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:16,292
grounded

27
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,080
in thinking about how quickly and how

28
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:23,280
thoroughly the economy is going to change.

29
00:02:23,758 --> 00:02:28,420
And we still have the same discussion that we should have about every technology.

30
00:02:28,878 --> 00:02:30,788
What's the best way of using it?

31
00:02:31,278 --> 00:02:31,680
How

32
00:02:32,846 --> 00:02:36,240
it could be misused, and who will benefit from it?

33
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:46,240
You mentioned agentic. Is there anything you're when you see it, you'll think, okay, now I can expand my figure to like two

34
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,188
digits uh when it comes to impact of AI?

35
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:50,020
I think

36
00:02:51,054 --> 00:02:58,116
the advances in coding have been really, really rapid. What is it about coding that makes it

37
00:02:59,280 --> 00:02:59,600
so

38
00:03:00,526 --> 00:03:04,964
conducive to have the most rapid advances of any occupation?

39
00:03:06,126 --> 00:03:10,020
I think it's the fact that coding is obviously done

40
00:03:10,958 --> 00:03:11,760
in offices.

41
00:03:11,982 --> 00:03:14,160
You don't have any interaction with the real world.

42
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,760
The people who are supervising coding are very, very

43
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:22,148
fluent in AI and in

44
00:03:23,086 --> 00:03:24,324
technology in general.

45
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:31,876
But also, and this is a very important part, just like math, just like chess, coding has rules.

46
00:03:32,142 --> 00:03:33,920
Something either runs or doesn't run.

47
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:40,964
Reinforcement learning is very effective when you have a ground truth that says, no, that's wrong. I think in many occupations we lack that.

48
00:03:41,358 --> 00:03:46,916
Customer service, I think most people would have said customer service is gonna be the first.

49
00:03:48,046 --> 00:03:49,092
Occupation to be

50
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:51,280
fully automated by AI.

51
00:03:51,534 --> 00:03:55,332
And we're seeing some of it, but it's turning out to be very slow and very difficult.

52
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,860
And most of the organizations that I deal with,

53
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:00,612
their AI system is so bad.

54
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:12,160
It's like a nightmare when I call customer service and I get AI. Oh my God, it's going to be another 10 minutes before somebody can actually help me. And that's because there's a social element.

55
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:17,760
Many of the things that you ask from customer service change over time.

56
00:04:18,094 --> 00:04:22,880
They are person-specific. Different people use different language for making the same questions.

57
00:04:23,694 --> 00:04:27,520
And there are a lot of gray areas, edge cases.

58
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:30,000
So that makes it much messier.

59
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:39,760
And it will require humans to either oversee it or work with it, or ideally, a much better collaboration

60
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:45,040
so that AI could be complementary to humans. So all of those are in play.

61
00:04:45,422 --> 00:04:48,480
Which ones we choose is going to have a very important impact.

62
00:04:48,846 --> 00:04:57,600
How many jobs are going to be displaced? How much productivity effects we're going to get, and who's going to benefit from it? With coding, we have deterministic outcome that we can check easily.

63
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:01,200
With customer service, it's much more personalized, as you mentioned.

64
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:10,560
Do you think in the future, with stronger models, we'll be able to achieve it, or it's just the bottleneck of AI in general that it can't function without a human in the

65
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:23,108
world? I would definitely not say there's this one thing that AI will not be able to do in the future. I think the last 10 years have given us enough examples of

66
00:05:24,078 --> 00:05:30,320
AI, if you put enough resources, enough data, can make progress on many things.

67
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:32,560
But the question is, how fast?

68
00:05:33,422 --> 00:05:36,000
And can it do so across all the domains?

69
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:43,120
if I step back and I say, if you ask me, why so little productivity improvements?

70
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:50,852
And what can we do about it? And I would have just one word for my vision of how we can improve

71
00:05:51,694 --> 00:05:54,532
the economic effects of AI, applications.

72
00:05:55,118 --> 00:06:05,760
We just need much better and more applications. And in some sense, one can make the argument, and again, I don't know. this may be incorrect. Look, I'm not in anthropic inside the organization.

73
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:17,440
I'm not in open AI. I don't know what they are learning in real time from their experiments and the enormous amount of resources, and they're very, very talented people in all of these organizations.

74
00:06:17,998 --> 00:06:22,372
But you can make the case that the United States,

75
00:06:23,118 --> 00:06:32,080
Silicon Valley, is putting too much emphasis on foundation models and not enough on applications, and not enough in integrating applications into organizations.

76
00:06:32,526 --> 00:06:32,960
So when

77
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:40,560
we're told all the time US is ahead of China. And indeed, in large language models, that seems quite clear.

78
00:06:40,910 --> 00:06:49,520
Chinese models are catching up sometimes, but they are using the lead of US models. There's some distilling going on, probably.

79
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:58,372
But one thing that I see Chinese companies do that I don't see as much in the United States is they're putting a lot of emphasis

80
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:02,640
in getting robotics, getting AI into the production line.

81
00:07:02,990 --> 00:07:06,628
And one thing that they are throwing at that problem

82
00:07:07,278 --> 00:07:09,316
is a huge amount of engineering talent.

83
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:13,040
The United States doesn't seem to have that much engineering talent.

84
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:16,000
And whatever we have is all going into foundation models.

85
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:25,280
You touched upon such an interesting subject because as an immigrant, when I see things, how they work in the US, and ask why is it

86
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,548
so, and the reply is, oh, historically.

87
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:33,252
Oh no, that's the only way to do it. I think here is one thing, one other thing I'll tell I'll say.

88
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:41,520
I don't want to make you know over generalizations, but I think as a signpost, if there is one thing that's been

89
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:46,960
not good for US AI industry in my opinion, it's the inevitabilism.

90
00:07:47,790 --> 00:07:51,460
That the view that whatever we're doing is the only way to do it.

91
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:53,120
There's no other way.

92
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:56,292
And if you're raising that question, you're an idiot.

93
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:08,260
So I completely reject that. I think AI is a very versatile technology, as are all general purpose technologies, GPTs, what economists call things like electricity,

94
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:15,360
printing press, etc., which spawn a lot of other applications.

95
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:27,440
One thing we know from history, one thing we've learned from a lot of economics and looking at previous examples, there are so many different ways of developing these general purpose technologies.

96
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:28,800
And different ways are not neutral.

97
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:32,960
They bring different types of benefits, they have different types of costs.

98
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:38,800
And just thinking that whatever we're doing is the only way, or by far the best way, I think is a mistake.

99
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:46,800
And I think another problem that comes with it is measurement, right? So how do you measure the impact of AI on your personal productivity, on your company's productivity?

100
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:50,884
And when I talked to Eric Björnerson when they were doing this study,

101
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:55,840
the economic impact of AI was basically zero. We talked to him in May.

102
00:08:56,160 --> 00:09:08,400
So the raw capabilities are skyrocketing, but the economic impact is pretty muted right now. That gap between the capabilities and what's actually happening is a big opportunity, I think.

103
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:17,440
Do you think there's a measurement problem there as well? He's a very, very serious scholar and he knows this industry pretty much better than any other social scientist.

104
00:09:17,678 --> 00:09:19,876
And I think he's more optimistic than I am.

105
00:09:20,334 --> 00:09:28,320
So if you ask him about the productivity effects in 2035, 2034, which is what I was writing about at the time,

106
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:31,200
he would have put it somewhat more aggressively.

107
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,348
But yeah, I think his conclusion is

108
00:09:34,062 --> 00:09:39,780
AI is not having the kinds of productivity effects we're expecting yet.

109
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:48,880
But it will, which is very much where I am as well. We don't see it yet, but it will. But it will be a little slower than, in my opinion, than what Eric would say.

110
00:09:49,166 --> 00:09:52,160
But I would be very, very happy if he's right.

111
00:09:52,494 --> 00:09:58,720
And I think he's got a much better chance of being right than many other people in the industry who are just overly optimistic about what's going on.

112
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:04,560
How do you think we should measure impact of AI on organization or even personal productivity?

113
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:09,360
Productivity has always been difficult to measure, and there's a quality issue.

114
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:11,200
I think one place where

115
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:21,840
Eric has put a lot of emphasis rightly is that in the digital age more broadly over the last 40 years, we don't know how to measure quality.

116
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:33,600
And that might mean that we are understating some of the productivity benefits of not just AI, but computers in general. I agree with that. The problem is I think we didn't know how to measure the

117
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:47,360
quality improvements coming from better cars or better highways either. So there would there always been an issue of measuring quality. I think it's the happiness measurement. Like

118
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:54,400
we should eventually just measure happiness and see why it's I mean that's also great. I think well-being is a very important thing.

119
00:10:54,640 --> 00:11:00,644
There, I would be more negative about AI and all the associated technologies. I think social media

120
00:11:01,582 --> 00:11:06,724
has been horrendous for us. It has damaged community, it has damaged people's

121
00:11:07,438 --> 00:11:14,404
ability to deal with adversity because we don't interact with people in real social networks.

122
00:11:15,534 --> 00:11:23,680
It has made our worst instincts more salient because algorithms go after rage, emotions,

123
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:24,160
envy.

124
00:11:24,366 --> 00:11:25,828
So I think it has been horrible.

125
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,880
I hope AI will not repeat social media's mistakes.

126
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:34,320
Let's take a quick break here because what Darren just said about technology really stood out to me.

127
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:43,440
AI can be used simply to replace tasks and cut costs, or it can help people become more productive and make better decisions.

128
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:54,800
As I see this all the time, running two companies and working with a big team, every new AI tool promises to save time. But adding more tools doesn't automatically make your business more efficient.

129
00:11:54,960 --> 00:12:03,200
If your marketing data is in one place, sales is working somewhere else, and customer information is spread across different systems,

130
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:10,160
your team ends up spending more time managing the tools instead of actually using them. That's what I like about HubSpot.

131
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:16,320
It brings marketing, sales, customer service, and content together on one AI-powered customer platform.

132
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:23,920
Your teams can work from the same customer information at every stage of the journey instead of constantly switching between disconnected systems.

133
00:12:24,206 --> 00:12:28,480
Each HubSpot product is powerful on its own, but they work even better together.

134
00:12:28,654 --> 00:12:36,400
So whether you're building a company or trying to make your existing team more effective, you can start with the tools you need and grow from there.

