00:15:00,846 --> 00:15:09,136
Again, discovered after I moved here, how much those people are making, how much you pay for a small and there's such a shortage of electricians. Oh yeah.

171
00:15:09,296 --> 00:15:17,316
And you wait three meet three weeks for the most basic task, and then an electrician comes and says, Oh no, this new machinery, I can't deal with it.

172
00:15:17,934 --> 00:15:18,336
Why not?

173
00:15:18,496 --> 00:15:19,536
Because I've never seen it before.

174
00:15:19,936 --> 00:15:22,116
this is where AI comes in. This is where AI comes in.

175
00:15:22,606 --> 00:15:23,776
It's just such an easy problem.

176
00:15:24,096 --> 00:15:29,056
When it comes to teachers and healthcare, do you think it's a government problem, or there's still opportunity for companies?

177
00:15:29,456 --> 00:15:38,576
Well, I think healthcare and education is a two-way street or three-way street, because most healthcare dollars have involvement from the government one way or another,

178
00:15:38,816 --> 00:15:39,620
teachers too.

179
00:15:40,336 --> 00:15:43,136
So yeah, I think the government could play an important role.

180
00:15:43,216 --> 00:15:52,004
But for electricians, blue-collar workers, for customer service representatives, for scientists, that's the private sector, mostly the private sector. So I said three-way because

181
00:15:52,846 --> 00:16:00,576
even when you take the government out, there are two parties, the tech companies and the management of the business that we're talking about.

182
00:16:00,846 --> 00:16:12,576
So both need to be on board. In my own conversations with CEOs of leading companies or high-level managers of leading companies, when I mention what I call pro-worker AI,

