00:17:00,014 --> 00:17:03,748
And one thing I see from their experience is that it's a

193
00:17:04,736 --> 00:17:13,616
Slow process and they require more engineers than they always think. Fortunately for them, China has an enormous supply of engineers.

194
00:17:14,016 --> 00:17:19,376
What would be your advice to entrepreneurs who are watching who are choosing what to work on? How do they choose the right niche?

195
00:17:19,536 --> 00:17:26,096
Because I think from all of the podcasts I've been doing recently, everyone keeps saying me, riches are in the niches, especially with AI,

196
00:17:26,176 --> 00:17:34,176
because opportunity lies in this boring business, which hasn't been automated in the past 20 years, and maybe it's still pen and paper.

197
00:17:34,496 --> 00:17:42,576
But if you come in and you tackle that problem, this is where the opportunity looks. Absolutely. And the only thing I would not have the the I would not have used the word boring.

198
00:17:42,656 --> 00:17:43,456
I think it's very exciting.

199
00:17:44,256 --> 00:17:48,176
boring from outside. AGI sounds a lot more. I know, I know, but

200
00:17:49,056 --> 00:17:52,164
that's so wrong. First of all, it is pie in the sky.

201
00:17:52,976 --> 00:17:56,416
Nobody can even agree on the definition of AGI.

202
00:17:56,590 --> 00:17:57,696
It's always in the future.

203
00:17:57,856 --> 00:18:02,660
And it is, frankly speaking, not a future that we can fully imagine either.

204
00:18:03,086 --> 00:18:11,536
I mean, if you really mean by AGI and then the ASI artificial superintelligence, machines could do everything, everything that humans can do

205
00:18:11,696 --> 00:18:12,016
better.

206
00:18:12,176 --> 00:18:15,536
That seems quite far to me. But it seems also very dystopian.

207
00:18:15,776 --> 00:18:23,972
I mean, there won't be even any jobs for the best physicists, the best mathematicians, the best social communicators, AI will do everything better.

208
00:18:24,622 --> 00:18:26,564
So, what I don't understand,

209
00:18:27,214 --> 00:18:31,296
there's so many like inconsistencies in the public debate on AI.

210
00:18:31,598 --> 00:18:38,816
One of them is people who they say, oh, AGI is great, it's going to come soon, but we're going to also have lots of things for humans to do.

211
00:18:39,056 --> 00:18:39,652
That can't be.

212
00:18:39,856 --> 00:18:43,524
If you mean anything serious by AGI or ASI,

213
00:18:44,398 --> 00:18:50,336
there can't be that many things left for humans. I wanted to dig a little deeper into the practical use of AI today.

214
00:18:50,416 --> 00:19:00,416
So when you mentioned that business opportunity researchers, how do you use AI in your daily research and what can be done? I must say I am behind the frontier.

215
00:19:00,576 --> 00:19:06,896
I use AI for background research, and I have increasingly over this year

216
00:19:08,016 --> 00:19:08,644
started

217
00:19:09,296 --> 00:19:19,456
consulting AI for criticism of things I write and ideas I have, which is very interesting because when I first experimented with this

218
00:19:19,616 --> 00:19:25,316
with ChatGPT 3.5 and then chatGPT 4, I thought it was very disappointing.

219
00:19:25,582 --> 00:19:35,056
And now it's so much better. I also encourage all of my students and research assistants to set up an AI infrastructure for their research and

220
00:19:36,176 --> 00:19:41,696
sort of as support for what they do, never to replace them. I discourage people,

221
00:19:42,496 --> 00:19:52,644
even people who have English as a second language, from using AI to write for them, because I think people should be in charge of their own writing.

222
00:19:52,896 --> 00:20:02,336
But I encourage every student who's a uh second uh is not a native speaker of English to use AI as a correction,

223
00:20:02,496 --> 00:20:12,416
like run it through AI and see where your sentences don't make sense or are too long or are non-grammatical. So I think there's a lot of mundane uses of AI already. That's very, very, very good.

224
00:20:12,576 --> 00:20:14,116
and there are lots of things, but

225
00:20:15,456 --> 00:20:23,716
I very much worry about a different kind of loss of control, which is that we collectively,

226
00:20:24,430 --> 00:20:33,284
but especially as students and learners, delegate too much to AI so that we don't flex our mental muscles, and we don't engage in learning.

227
00:20:33,870 --> 00:20:44,576
That is, I think, a first order issue. I I see that a lot with students from studies that I think the use of AI has been pretty disastrous in

228
00:20:44,942 --> 00:20:48,976
K 12 education because we've done it blindly.

229
00:20:50,030 --> 00:20:51,184
without guardrails.

230
00:20:51,424 --> 00:21:04,944
I said that actually in 2024 also, or 2023, that it was a dangerous, huge social experiment to unleash generative AI on schools without any guardrails, all at the same time.

231
00:21:05,198 --> 00:21:07,504
Because we didn't know what it was going to do.

232
00:21:07,790 --> 00:21:13,584
And I don't think it has my worst fears have come to pass, but it's been pretty close to that.

233
00:21:14,144 --> 00:21:23,824
When do you need to say to yourself, okay, I'm going to stop here and I need to think by myself when it comes to this issue and just use AI maybe to

234
00:21:24,784 --> 00:21:35,184
I mean because for me it's like different. First of all, I'm from a different generation, unfortunately. And second, I just love thinking, I love taking pen and paper and sketching things out.

235
00:21:35,424 --> 00:21:37,860
So in fact, I have to prompt myself,

236
00:21:38,798 --> 00:21:43,844
run this through AI. I have never used, for example, AI to do math. I should.

237
00:21:44,782 --> 00:21:48,516
I mean, AI is much better than me in math, but I like doing the math myself.

238
00:21:48,814 --> 00:21:58,544
When I write down models or when I do calculations, I do them by hand because that's what I'm used to, and I can do it fast and I can learn something. I learned something from my mistakes.

239
00:21:58,704 --> 00:22:02,224
But I'm sure if I step back, I could do more.

240
00:22:02,624 --> 00:22:05,184
When you do math for yourself, do you ever think about this?

241
00:22:05,424 --> 00:22:12,544
It was the calculator problem because teachers were protesting against the calculator. Now we still use it everywhere, and it sped up the complexity of things that we can solve.

242
00:22:12,704 --> 00:22:21,184
Do you think AI is going to enable us to do that? Or because it's taking away in general a critical thinking, we won't be able to progress further. I worry about the latter.

243
00:22:21,504 --> 00:22:31,812
I think the analogy to the calculator is useful, but you have to do it carefully. The calculator could only take over some very, very, very small part of it.

244
00:22:32,304 --> 00:22:37,984
And I know of nobody who used a calculator to sort of do thinking for them.

245
00:22:38,158 --> 00:22:47,344
And I know of no school ever that said to students, now you have a calculator, you don't learn how to multiply. I mean, there were calculators when I was a student.

246
00:22:47,886 --> 00:22:55,264
We all learned how to multiply, how to divide. And I can't imagine not knowing that. That is such an important building block for many other things.

247
00:22:55,470 --> 00:22:58,404
Same thing for differentiation. Like, if you don't know calculus,

248
00:22:59,374 --> 00:23:09,424
how could you even take the next step? Yeah, it's how far the skill goes. With calculators, basically just this function. With AI, it's the whole critical thing. It's the whole critical thinking.

249
00:23:10,064 --> 00:23:13,060
and the boundaries are very gray.

250
00:23:13,806 --> 00:23:15,344
Like, where do you

251
00:23:16,304 --> 00:23:18,276
stop in using AI?

252
00:23:18,544 --> 00:23:20,304
How do you still

253
00:23:21,184 --> 00:23:28,804
engage in the key steps of human innovation and human creativity together with AI?

254
00:23:29,184 --> 00:23:30,704
One thing before we keep going.

255
00:23:30,864 --> 00:23:39,984
If you want to stay updated on all the things these founders say here, we take the use cases and prompts from every conversation and put them into my newsletter called Future Proof.

256
00:23:40,064 --> 00:23:50,704
And of course, we also put my own beautiful use cases there. For example, we'll put together the seven skills that came up again and again, the ones people will need in the next few years.

257
00:23:50,944 --> 00:23:53,904
These are the skills that my team and I are also using daily.

258
00:23:54,144 --> 00:23:55,984
Subscribe through the link in the description.

259
00:23:56,144 --> 00:23:57,264
You'll get that one.

260
00:23:57,454 --> 00:24:01,104
And you can also read our previous newsletters and see what you missed.

261
00:24:01,344 --> 00:24:02,864
Going back to businesses.

262
00:24:03,024 --> 00:24:12,944
So when you look at all those posts on X where someone's saying, you know, I haven't seen a lot of like I fired my whole team and hired agents, but I've seen a lot of posts where people say,

263
00:24:13,104 --> 00:24:20,304
Oh, instead of hiring someone, I deployed an agent. What do these companies and people miss in the long term when they do that?

264
00:24:20,464 --> 00:24:26,864
Because I know you're against marketing agents as a replacement for a person. Let me actually clarify.

265
00:24:27,790 --> 00:24:36,356
My stance on this. And this is based you know on work I've done on robots, work I've done on

266
00:24:37,070 --> 00:24:43,748
early 20th century technology, work I've done on the Industrial Revolution, and theoretical work I've done.

267
00:24:44,032 --> 00:24:48,804
I am not against automation, nor do I think it's possible to stop automation.

268
00:24:49,422 --> 00:24:50,404
We want automation.

269
00:24:50,992 --> 00:24:54,884
And anybody who wants to reverse automation, I'm against that.

270
00:24:55,438 --> 00:24:59,312
We don't want to go back to the days where we carry heavy things on our backs.

271
00:24:59,502 --> 00:25:00,708
Automation is great.

272
00:25:01,294 --> 00:25:11,012
The problem is if the only thing we do is automation, A, we're not going to get the full productivity because it's not that easy to do everything with automation.

273
00:25:11,232 --> 00:25:22,500
And B, we're not going to create shared prosperity because that will eliminate too many jobs without creating meaningful, high-paying, high productivity jobs to create new opportunities for people.

274
00:25:23,072 --> 00:25:29,380
So my point has always been use new technology both for automation and other things. Have a balanced approach.

275
00:25:29,872 --> 00:25:40,672
And digital technologies, I have argued, have provided a lot of opportunities for generating new things for humans to do. And it's doubly true with AI.

276
00:25:40,752 --> 00:25:50,592
So I want AI to be used for automation, so long as we also use AI for creating new things, like for nurses, for electricians, for many occupations we can't even dream about right now.

277
00:25:50,912 --> 00:25:55,872
How do you explain it to a small business owner whose only metric is increasing profits?

278
00:25:56,142 --> 00:25:57,732
What is the economic downside?

279
00:25:58,832 --> 00:26:02,912
Productivity. I think again, and and my experience, again, it may be cheap talk.

280
00:26:03,118 --> 00:26:06,432
People may be nice to me when I talk to them, or I might have fooled them.

281
00:26:07,086 --> 00:26:08,832
But most businesses actually get that.

282
00:26:08,992 --> 00:26:14,512
Not everybody, but most of them get that. Your workers are your greatest asset. Imagine making them more productive.

283
00:26:14,734 --> 00:26:16,192
That's fantastic for the business.

284
00:26:16,398 --> 00:26:18,340
And in fact, it's a better calculus.

285
00:26:18,912 --> 00:26:31,472
Like, imagine you have 100 employees, and I tell you, you can automate 5% of those jobs and reduce the cost by half in those 5%. Or you can make your workers 5% more productive.

286
00:26:31,662 --> 00:26:39,872
The latter is going to get you much more. Oh, and your competitors are going to be automating their workforce. And if you're getting rid of people, then your productivity is going to eventually.

287
00:26:40,672 --> 00:26:40,992
I mean,

288
00:26:42,382 --> 00:26:47,792
it's just your workers have a lot of experience, have a lot of some of them. If you've been a good boss,

289
00:26:48,622 --> 00:26:50,992
they have commitment to the work or to the to the to the business.

290
00:26:51,152 --> 00:26:59,792
If you can make them more innovative, better at problem solving, better at doing new things, that's the kind of business that's going to succeed. Let me ask you a question.

291
00:26:59,952 --> 00:27:04,516
What are the companies, which ones are the companies from the past that you remember?

292
00:27:04,832 --> 00:27:10,352
And you'll give me familiar answers like Ford Motor Company, General Motors, General Electric.

293
00:27:12,672 --> 00:27:14,672
IBM, Microsoft.

294
00:27:15,054 --> 00:27:25,792
What's common about all of them did something new. They didn't just cut costs and take took what others were doing, they imagined new markets, new technologies, new tasks.

295
00:27:26,032 --> 00:27:31,632
Henry Ford himself captured that. He said, if I had asked the consumers what they wanted, they would have told me faster horses.

296
00:27:31,792 --> 00:27:34,052
So that's what we need from successful companies.

297
00:27:34,272 --> 00:27:39,312
And cutting your cost by 5%, that's not going to make you go down the Hindu history books.

298
00:27:39,632 --> 00:27:51,072
When it comes to automation, do you have a rule on how do you know which task? You mentioned 5% of tasks are automatable. How do you know when to automate something or when it's not worth automating?

299
00:27:51,392 --> 00:27:59,312
If you cut your cost by 5%, GDP is typically going to go up by 5% times your share of GDP.

300
00:27:59,472 --> 00:28:05,552
That's not hard. The hard part is A, if you're changing quality, that's a little bit more complicated.

301
00:28:05,712 --> 00:28:09,232
And B, actually, even if you're going to cut costs by 5%.

302
00:28:10,606 --> 00:28:14,064
In a number of years, it's going to take you a lot of work to get there.

303
00:28:14,224 --> 00:28:22,944
And what I'm seeing from a few businesses that I know is that they take the automation plunge sometimes because they think it's going to be easy,

304
00:28:23,024 --> 00:28:26,372
sometimes because they think they're under pressure, and then they can't make it work.

305
00:28:26,734 --> 00:28:28,384
Because automation is not that easy.

306
00:28:28,686 --> 00:28:30,384
You have to integrate it into the company.

307
00:28:30,624 --> 00:28:42,596
Now, many people have started using the word AI has a jagged frontier. I like that image, but it's also a code word for saying, oh, we overestimated AI.

308
00:28:42,944 --> 00:28:44,704
It's much harder to do it.

309
00:28:45,024 --> 00:28:47,460
And we thought we could lay off workers

310
00:28:48,142 --> 00:28:49,504
because of AI. No, we can't.

311
00:28:49,664 --> 00:28:54,064
But if you take that really seriously, it means, oh, it's so jagged.

312
00:28:54,190 --> 00:28:58,864
I'm going to keep more or less the same number of workers. And now I'm doing AI and it's just a melange.

313
00:28:59,184 --> 00:29:03,104
Yeah, it's just a lot more work. From businesses, moving on to education.

314
00:29:03,502 --> 00:29:08,544
As someone who's in academia, is university still going to be worth a lot of money in 10 years?

315
00:29:08,944 --> 00:29:09,664
I think so.

316
00:29:09,904 --> 00:29:12,704
But let me preface that with a few caveats.

317
00:29:13,678 --> 00:29:21,104
First, I have never believed, and I'm even more convinced about this, both for economic and social reasons, that everybody should go to college.

318
00:29:21,294 --> 00:29:24,292
People have diverse skills, they have different interests.

319
00:29:24,718 --> 00:29:27,104
We should be engaged in lifelong learning.

320
00:29:27,438 --> 00:29:36,784
But the traditional liberal arts type education is not for everyone, nor do we want everybody to have exactly the same perspective in society.

321
00:29:36,944 --> 00:29:41,984
Second, a lot of what university does, I'm afraid to say, is also a signal.

322
00:29:42,126 --> 00:29:45,476
When you go to Harvard, MIT, Stanford, you're also buying the name.

323
00:29:45,584 --> 00:29:49,424
And that's good for you, not necessarily that good for society.

324
00:29:49,584 --> 00:30:00,164
And third, every university is understanding now that they have to change their business model, they have to change how they deliver education. They are in search of

325
00:30:00,878 --> 00:30:03,876
what types of skills they should focus on.

326
00:30:04,064 --> 00:30:04,704
And my

327
00:30:05,422 --> 00:30:09,664
very, very superficial opinion is that universities understand that.

328
00:30:10,190 --> 00:30:10,784
But you know what?

329
00:30:11,182 --> 00:30:14,144
The same actually applies to high schools and middle schools, and they are not.

330
00:30:14,224 --> 00:30:16,144
They're even further behind the curve.

331
00:30:16,304 --> 00:30:22,276
One thing I have experienced in the United States is that many students, when they come to college,

332
00:30:23,054 --> 00:30:23,904
are unprepared.

333
00:30:24,064 --> 00:30:25,892
And that is going to get worse.

334
00:30:26,894 --> 00:30:30,784
And that really will make colleges' business even harder.

335
00:30:31,264 --> 00:30:44,144
I've seen that, because I have, you know, written a textbook for first years in economics, how often weakly prepared students are, and how that's actually getting worse over time, even before AI;

336
00:30:44,384 --> 00:30:53,504
how many colleges are finding it necessary to do more and more remedial stuff because high schools aren't doing well enough.

337
00:30:53,664 --> 00:31:01,184
And the evidence we have, I mean, we are in the early days, things can change, but the evidence is that

338
00:31:01,454 --> 00:31:06,624
AI has made learning harder in middle schools and high schools, and so that problem is going to get worse.

339
00:31:07,024 --> 00:31:16,144
Totally. If you start using AI to solve all the problems, and then university is only going to get worse then. So, what should people be doing? What skills should they be acquiring?

340
00:31:16,304 --> 00:31:23,664
And do you agree with the notion that we'd need even more academic knowledge to acquire those skills? Because I'm thinking about taste and strategic thinking. Absolutely.

341
00:31:23,904 --> 00:31:31,424
Because I don't believe true AGI is just around the corner, I think we need human creativity more than ever.

342
00:31:32,302 --> 00:31:36,516
And human creativity without strong foundations is an oxymoron.

343
00:31:36,784 --> 00:31:43,424
If anybody wants my opinion, and I'm not the most qualified to give you this opinion, I'm not a mathematician and I'm not a computer scientist.

344
00:31:43,918 --> 00:31:52,868
But if anybody wants my opinion, I would say go and study math. Don't be put off by Astra is solving difficult math problems.

345
00:31:53,294 --> 00:31:58,944
We need human mathematical knowledge, we need human mathematical maturity. That's going to be so important.

346
00:31:59,184 --> 00:32:05,892
Go and study physics, go and study economics, go and study engineering. I think those are going to be great. But also learn flexibility.

347
00:32:06,414 --> 00:32:16,864
I think we're going to need a lot of flexibility because even within the same occupation, we will often need to change what we do as the technology changes, as the social context changes.

348
00:32:17,422 --> 00:32:19,664
So I think those are really, really important things.

349
00:32:20,046 --> 00:32:20,304
But

350
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:24,356
Human ingenuity and human creativity,

351
00:32:25,166 --> 00:32:28,560
I think, are going to be critical in the next 10 years.

352
00:32:28,880 --> 00:32:36,960
Yeah, and normalizing nonlinear careers, something that resonated with me a lot, someone who changed careers throughout our life. I think this is going to be our new normal.

353
00:32:37,200 --> 00:32:44,452
But then two other principles that I think are very important that we have a day we should impart, starting from middle school.

354
00:32:45,230 --> 00:32:48,516
Learn how to work alongside and with AI.

355
00:32:49,166 --> 00:32:50,212
Absolutely.

356
00:32:50,702 --> 00:32:54,960
But also even more emphasis on civic responsibility.

357
00:32:55,200 --> 00:33:04,292
We have lost in this country and in most of the rest of the world a true sense of community and a true sense of

358
00:33:04,800 --> 00:33:09,360
caring for the common good and working for our collective good.

359
00:33:09,550 --> 00:33:15,200
And this is even more important in the age of AI. And that starts with being informed, being engaged.

360
00:33:15,600 --> 00:33:22,560
That starts with elementary school, really. You know, it starts with elementary school. I I yeah, I could have extended to elementary school, absolutely.

361
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:25,124
We may have encouraged too much selfishness.

362
00:33:26,094 --> 00:33:31,840
We may have encouraged people to draw into their cocoons via social media and other things.

363
00:33:32,174 --> 00:33:36,560
We may have denigrated some aspects of community that are really important.

364
00:33:36,960 --> 00:33:47,040
That's also like a major theme of my new book on what happened to liberal democracy: that you really need community to be at the center of a liberal democratic society.

365
00:33:47,214 --> 00:33:51,268
You know, here's what I am not pessimist, I'm not an optimist.

366
00:33:51,566 --> 00:33:58,084
I'm not but I'm definitely not a pessimist either. I try to remain hopeful. And two things I find hope in

367
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:02,080
are that humans are infinitely adaptable.

368
00:34:02,286 --> 00:34:05,380
And second, even in this polarized, divided world,

369
00:34:06,446 --> 00:34:08,100
we have very similar

370
00:34:09,198 --> 00:34:14,436
notions of fairness, moral shared understanding on both sides of the aisle.

371
00:34:15,022 --> 00:34:22,564
They just need to come up more clearly in our communication, and we need to see the other side not as the enemy.

372
00:34:23,406 --> 00:34:25,604
Even the deepest disagreements

373
00:34:26,800 --> 00:34:31,360
should be situated within a context in which we recognize we're on the same boat.

374
00:34:31,680 --> 00:34:40,240
We're talking about all of this, but then there comes Dario and says there's a 25% chance things go really, really badly with AI.

375
00:34:40,560 --> 00:34:49,600
I'm relatively an optimist, so I think there's a 25% chance that things go really, really badly, and a 75% chance that things go really, really well with

376
00:34:49,920 --> 00:34:51,760
not much uh not much space.

377
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:59,040
What do you think when you hear this, especially coming from someone who has to IPO soon and further technologies, very transformational?

378
00:34:59,360 --> 00:35:04,452
I think if people in the leading AI labs are worried about

379
00:35:05,294 --> 00:35:09,156
some of the implications of AI, of course they should voice that.

380
00:35:09,486 --> 00:35:11,172
I welcome and I applaud

381
00:35:12,110 --> 00:35:14,320
their courage to speak.

382
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:22,628
But I am myself much more worried about a general sense of loss of control of society over AI

383
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:32,400
than existential risk, as articulated by Dario or by Elon Musk or

384
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:34,372
by Claxon.

385
00:35:34,560 --> 00:35:40,260
Loss of control I'm talking about is that the general public, the workers, students,

386
00:35:41,006 --> 00:35:46,628
parents, lose control of their own agency because of AI.

387
00:35:47,360 --> 00:35:49,188
Not necessarily

388
00:35:50,030 --> 00:35:58,212
because AI has become super intelligent and an entity unto itself, but partly because of the way that other companies

389
00:35:59,054 --> 00:36:04,080
or frontier labs are developing AI, are using AI, and are rolling out AI.

390
00:36:04,240 --> 00:36:10,800
So I am very worried about loss of control. We should have that conversation, but we should not ignore other risks.

391
00:36:11,200 --> 00:36:16,452
You have this rating for AI going from minus 10 to plus 10, and you said it was minus six.

392
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:25,040
Did it get worse from then I think it was 2025 when you gave this rating? No, I actually don't think so, because in 2024, 2025.

393
00:36:26,318 --> 00:36:29,072
I thought there was a lot of damage that AI could do.

394
00:36:29,232 --> 00:36:37,764
And I was not clear about what bottlenecks would be facing us in improving it. I think the improvements have been fast.

395
00:36:37,872 --> 00:36:43,684
So the dangers have increased. But I think the good things we can do with AI are also becoming clear.

396
00:36:43,952 --> 00:36:47,108
But we just need to redirect it. We need to steer AI.

397
00:36:47,792 --> 00:36:56,260
And AI cannot just be the decision of a handful of people, very smart, many of them very well-meaning people.

398
00:36:57,072 --> 00:37:00,592
But it's too important to be left to just a few people.

399
00:37:00,912 --> 00:37:03,072
How do you foster that on an individual level?

400
00:37:03,392 --> 00:37:03,952
Information.

401
00:37:04,270 --> 00:37:06,884
I think we have to start with an information and a narrative.

